{"name":"Pale Cornflower","hex":"#ced9e1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Light in color.","Having a pallor (a light color, especially due to sickness, shock, fright etc.)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To turn pale; to lose colour.","To become insignificant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pale"},{"term":"cornflower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small annual plant in the family Asteraceae, Centaurea cyanus, usually with bushy blue flowers which grows natively in European cornfields (i.e. wheatfields).","A plant of the species Cichorium intybus."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Synonym of cornflower blue, a shade of blue similar to that of the usual cornflower."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cornflower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Centaurea cyanus","blocks":[{"text":"Centaurea cyanus, commonly known as cornflower or bachelor's button, is an annual flowering plant in the family Asteraceae native to Europe. In the past, it often grew as a weed in cornfields, hence its name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Centaurea_cyanus_flower_001.jpg/330px-Centaurea_cyanus_flower_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centaurea_cyanus","wikidata":"Q156921"},{"title":"Cornflower blue","blocks":[{"text":"Cornflower blue is a shade of medium-to-light blue containing relatively little green. This hue was one of the favorites of the Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/CentaureaCyanus-bloem-kl.jpg/330px-CentaureaCyanus-bloem-kl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornflower_blue","wikidata":"Q2021643"},{"title":"Colorfulness","blocks":[{"text":"Colorfulness, chroma and saturation are attributes of perceived color relating to chromatic intensity. As defined formally by the International Commission on Illumination (CIE) they respectively describe three different aspects of chromatic intensity, but the terms are often used loosely and interchangeably in contexts where these aspects are not clearly distinguished. The precise meanings of the terms vary by what other functions they are dependent on."},{"items":["Colorfulness is the \"attribute of a visual perception according to which the perceived color of an area appears to be more or less chromatic \". The colorfulness evoked by an object depends not only on its spectral reflectance but also on the strength of the illumination, and increases with the latter unless the brightness is very high.","Chroma is the \"colorfulness of an area judged as a proportion of the brightness of a similarly illuminated area that appears white or highly transmitting\". As a result, chroma is mostly only dependent on the spectral properties, and as such is seen to describe the object color. It is how different from a grey of the same lightness such an object color appears to be.","Saturation is the \"colorfulness of an area judged in proportion to its brightness\", which in effect is the perceived freedom from whitishness of the light coming from the area. An object with a given spectral reflectance exhibits approximately constant saturation for all levels of illumination, unless the brightness is very high."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Brightness_and_colorfulness.png/330px-Brightness_and_colorfulness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colorfulness","wikidata":"Q27673"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Centaurea_cyanus_-_Keila.jpg/500px-Centaurea_cyanus_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Centaurea_cyanus_-_Keila.jpg/1280px-Centaurea_cyanus_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Centaurea_cyanus_-_Keila.jpg/500px-Centaurea_cyanus_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACentaurea_cyanus_-_Keila.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Centaurea_cyanus_3.jpg/500px-Centaurea_cyanus_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Centaurea_cyanus_3.jpg/1280px-Centaurea_cyanus_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Centaurea_cyanus_3.jpg/500px-Centaurea_cyanus_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Introduced specimen near Peshastin, Chelan County, Washington","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACentaurea_cyanus_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/EB_1911_Flowering_shoot_of_Cornflower.png/500px-EB_1911_Flowering_shoot_of_Cornflower.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/EB_1911_Flowering_shoot_of_Cornflower.png/500px-EB_1911_Flowering_shoot_of_Cornflower.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flowering shoot; I. Disk-floret in vertical section","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEB_1911_Flowering_shoot_of_Cornflower.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Apis_mellifera%2C_France.jpg/500px-Apis_mellifera%2C_France.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co